hi there steve kaufman here  um i said i think in an answer by the  way uh please subscribe to my channel if  you enjoy uh hearing and talking about  the issues surrounding language learning  and if you like this video give it a  like  uh today i want to talk about three  months because three months comes up  quite often  we have our 90-day challenges at link i  have done quite a few i've done it in  polish in  korean in  arabic and persian and maybe some other  i can't remember  uh three months we had the three months  to fluency of that  you know benny the irish polyglot spoke  of  it's a period of time where we can sort  of focus our energies and hopefully  achieve something  so i want to talk a bit about that i had  said that i was going to talk about  learning you know more than one language  at the same time or that i was going to  talk about you know getting to speaking  and and both of those things i want to  talk about but i want to talk about them  next thursday and i want to have a live  streaming session such as we used to  have so that means that we'll go on for  45 minutes so however long  you will sign up and we'll get the  information out to you on on how you can  sign up and you can post your questions  and i will answer those questions live  and i want to focus it on the idea of  sort of learning and maintaining several  languages and the issue of getting to  speaking and we can talk about those  things at that  live streaming event now  the three months so what can we achieve  in three months and and how should we  approach  uh sort of a three-month period of time  so first of all i will sort of recount  for you sort of my recent  experience in sort of three months okay  so i my wife and i went to israel  okay so i got this t-shirt  from the hebrew university  in tel aviv  and i put in probably  three weeks prior to going there into  hebrew  uh i would say that my hebrew is  non-existent okay even trying to read it  whatever i did i've essentially  forgotten so with hebrew i only put in a  few weeks  uh if i start up again  i'm almost starting at the beginning  i'll be a little bit better because i'll  be refreshing things that i tried to do  and so it'll come back faster and  stronger but i have essentially zero  there okay but as a result of  going to israel then my wife and i went  into jordan and we visited petra  and  that made me think um  really there's an awful lot more arabic  speakers than hebrew speakers i'm going  to learn arabic so as a result of  starting to learn hebrew i was motivated  to learn arabic and this comes back to a  point that i always have experienced in  my life and that is you never know  what's going to lead to what one and one  doesn't always equal two the main thing  is to actually do something and if you  do something good things will happen  so i then went into arabic and i spent  you know off and on because i was  traveling in latin america i was  traveling in ukraine and stuff but i put  in about seven or eight months into  arabic  then i said  you know i have now learned to read  arabic more or less  and there's a lot of uh persian speakers  farsi speakers here in  vancouver why don't i learn  persian so i spent essentially three  months  with persian at the end of which i did a  brief  um  video  and then  my wife is watching turkish  soap operas on netflix so i said hey i'd  like to understand what they're saying  so i got into turkish and i've now spent  about two months on turkish so here's my  feeling about three months  three months is a good period especially  if you are starting  a new language now anyone that is  starting a new language  or has studied other languages is  already by definition  uh an enjoyment learner okay that's  different from a person who needs  english for their job a person who needs  english for their job they're going to  be totally focused on english but in my  case i learn it because it's enjoyable  and what do i achieve in three months  well first of all how so i avoid  too much time on grammar explanations  because i don't have any sense of the  language i certainly never do grammar  exercises i focus entirely on listening  and reading and i focus on  the many stories because the first time  i listen i'm just accumulating words  and and the first time i study these  lessons i'm accumulating words and only  slowly do i start to notice  the different forms the different  functions of the different forms and  even today while cleaning up after  breakfast  i noticed certain things about turkish  verbs that i hadn't noticed before  and however the other day  driving to the office i put in my arabic  you know i listened to arabic  and i listened to the  persian coming back from the office  i heard the words more clearly than i  did before  in other words spending time with  turkish  has  increased my  ability to clearly hear these other  languages and i've always found that  studying new languages was kind of like  a an exercise for the brain which uh  improves your ability to hear  other languages however obviously i have  slipped  i've forgotten things  so  i kind of end up with sort of the  following  feeling about the first three months of  a language okay you cannot achieve  fluency in three months because  fluency requires lots of practice using  the using the language speaking the  language eventually you have to speak a  lot you have to speak in meaning of  meaningful situations not just uh hello  how are you where's the bathroom but  talking about things of interest a wide  range of subjects lots of speaking until  you have had that you will not be fluent  so three months of study after three  months you don't have the vocabulary to  engage in meaningful conversation and  therefore it's not  practical to think that you can be  fluent in three months however  i have a level in arabic  turkish even  and persian  that i don't slip that much i listen to  my arabic i listen to my  uh persian i'm aware of certain  structures in the language i'm aware of  vocabulary there  i've slipped some  when i go back to doing those languages  and go back to some of the same content  some of the same many stories  refresh what's in my brain you know i  get right back to where i was and go  forward  so the three-month period gives you that  toehold  the language is now no longer strange  you have a certain amount of vocabulary  some of which you'll forget but which  you can quickly relearn  the culture and the people  are no longer strange  uh if i  even if i watch people speak arabic and  and and farsi and turkish and i don't  understand what they're saying i have a  sense of who they are i have a sense of  that culture so i have this ability now  by having my sort of arabic was eight  months but three months on persian two  months on turkish i've discovered this  whole new world so the goal of the first  three months  is not to achieve fluency because  even when for example as a spanish  speaker i learned  portuguese  uh i i put in more than three months  uh and this was before we had portuguese  at link so i was using living language  and stuff like that but still went to  portugal i was dead like people just  switched back to english they didn't  have patience for me i had to put in  more time then i went to portugal and  then slowly i started getting enough  interaction with people to where i can  even today i don't really call myself  tremendously fluent in portuguese  because i mix in so much spanish or  italian or whatever so three months is a  key period to  first of all get that sense that you can  learn the language get us some sense of  achievement  some sense of familiarity with the  language  don't worry about how correctly you use  the language it won't matter until  you've had you know come across these  same words and phrases in different  situations and you start to notice the  function of different words and then you  can of course look up grammar when  